    "result_text": "The project contains only the `AcceptanceTest` class, and the build succeeds with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in the owner-creation flow.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.java` (the `POST /api/owners` handler's `toE164` normalization):\n\n1. Added a lookup table mapping country calling codes to their required national-digit counts:\n   ```java\n   private static final Map<String, Integer> NATIONAL_DIGITS_BY_COUNTRY = Map.of(\"1\", 10, \"61\", 9);\n   ```\n\n2. Extended `toE164(...)` to reject (return `null`, which the controller already maps to **400 Bad Request**) any number whose national-number length doesn't match its country code, via a new `hasValidNationalLength(...)` helper. It uses longest-prefix matching so a recognised code is validated exactly (`+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10), while unrecognised country codes fall back to the pre-existing general 8\u201315 digit bound.\n\n3. Added the `java.util.Map` and `java.util.Comparator` imports and updated the Javadoc.\n\n**Verification:**\n- The new test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (posts `\"+61 123\"`, expects 400) passes.\n- The valid-owner path is preserved: `uniqueTelephone()` produces `04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 normalizes to `+61` + 9 national digits, which satisfies the new rule.\n- Full `./mvnw test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, no failures.",
